course: dictionary
lesson: 525
title: Words that show surprise
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Astonishingly is an adverb. An adverb tells you how something happens or how strong something is. «Some animals and plants live astonishingly long.» The word long tells us how they live, and astonishingly tells us how much.
explain: You can check the meaning by swapping in a word you already know. «Live very long» still makes sense, so astonishingly means very or extremely. It also carries surprise, so it means in a way that causes great surprise.
explain: Look at the clues around it. The clam from Iceland lived about five hundred years, older than Shakespeare, and bristlecone pines have grown for nearly five thousand years. Numbers that big make us gasp, and that is why the writer chose astonishingly.
fact: Astonishingly is an adverb, so it describes how something happens or how something is.
fact: «Astonishingly» means in a way that causes great surprise.
fact: «Astonishingly» can also mean very or extremely, as in «astonishingly fast».
fact: You will often see it in stories, reports and nature facts, with phrases such as «astonishingly, …» and «astonishingly well».
exercise: «Some animals and plants live astonishingly long.» What does astonishingly mean here? | Only just | *Very, extremely | Quietly | Never
exercise: Which phrase means the same as «astonishingly fast»? | Slowly and carefully | Not fast at all | *Extremely fast | Fast and quiet
exercise: Sam says astonishingly means boring. What does astonishingly really mean? | A kind of animal | Sleepy and tired | Boring and dull | *In a way that causes great surprise
test: «Her answers were astonishingly well done.» Which meaning fits astonishingly well? | Once in a while | *Extremely well | Not at all | Badly
test: The clam lived about five hundred years, older than Shakespeare. Which clue shows the age is surprising? | It lived in Iceland | *It was older than Shakespeare | It was a clam | It lived a long time ago
exam: What does astonishingly mean in «an astonishingly long life»? | Quietly, like a whisper | Tiredly | Badly | *In a way that causes great surprise
